 # Ambient Occlusion via Geometry/Neighbors
 
-Also called: Local Geometry-Based Ambient Occlusion, Voxel Neighbor Occlusion
+Also called: Local Ambient Occlusion, Voxel AO, SDF-based AO
 
 ## Overview
-This family of methods estimates local ambient occlusion by querying the immediate geometric neighborhood of a point—typically via Signed Distance Fields (SDF), voxel occupancy grids, or vertex neighborhoods—to approximate visibility without explicit ray tracing against triangle meshes or screen-space depth buffers [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [JAOCC Shading](3634-jaocc-shading.md) and [Local Ambient Occlusion](4977-local-ambient-occlusion.md) by directly evaluating geometric distance or discrete neighbor occupancy to determine occlusion factors, rather than relying on stochastic sampling or precomputed global buffers [G].
+This family of methods estimates local ambient occlusion (AO) in volume rendering by querying geometric representations—such as Signed Distance Fields (SDFs), discrete voxel grids, or local density distributions—to approximate visibility without performing expensive global ray tracing against triangle meshes or screen-space depth buffers [S1, S2, S3, S4, 2527-ambient-occlusion-via-sdf-geometry.md, 6646-voxel-ambient-occlusion.md]. Unlike [JAOCC Shading](3634-jaocc-shading.md) which uses fixed cubic neighborhoods, or [Local Ambient Occlusion](4977-local-ambient-occlusion.md) which integrates transmittance along stochastic rays, these methods determine occlusion factors by directly evaluating geometric distance, neighbor occupancy, or gradient-weighted density, offering a computationally cheaper alternative suitable for real-time or interactive applications [G].
 
